s no entropy floor for custom rules (unlike the built-in entropy detector at `redact/redact.go:189` which requires Shannon entropy > 4.5). A rule that matches the literal word `damn` will redact `damn`.\n- **The `samples[]` self-test won't stop it.** Sample mismatches log a `slog.Warn` but never drop the rule (`redact/custom.go:96`). Even an obviously-not-secret regex loads fine.\n- **Output is indistinguishable from a real redaction.** All custom rules emit the bare `REDACTED` token; the rule's `label`/`id` is kept only for diagnostic logs, never the output (`redact/custom.go:24`).\n- **Scope is the entire transcript pipeline.** `redact.String` runs over JSONL transcript content before checkpointing (`redact/redact.go:166`), so user prompts, agent text, and tool args would all be filtered. The user's own keystrokes get scrubbed before the data ever lands in `entire/checkpoints/v1`.\n\n### Caveats worth noting in review\n\n- **Naming is misleading.** Both surfaces are called \"custom_secrets\" / \"redactors\" and the docs frame this as a credential-shape feature (PR description: \"internal credential shapes and custom token prefixes\"). The mechanism is generic, but users following the docs would not think to use it for content moderation. If anything, that's an argument for renaming the surface to `custom_redactors` rather than `custom_secrets` so the broader scope is honest.\n- **No allowlist of \"what this is for.\"** There's nothing stopping a misguided team from writing a regex that redacts, e.g., a project codename or an exec's name. That's not a *bug*, but it is a policy/governance question the PR doesn't address.\n- **`docs/security-and-privacy.md` should probably acknowledge this.** It currently frames custom rules as a secret-redaction extension. A one-line note that \"rules are arbitrary regex and can be used to redact any string pattern, not just secrets\" would set correct expectations.\n\n### Branch status\n\n- **Updated with main:** Merged `origin/mai